<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Galeshewe</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1840–<a href="/years/1927">1927</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Kgosi Galeshewe</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Galeshewe was a chief of the Batlhaping in the northern Cape, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, who resisted colonial expansion. He took part in a rising in 1878 and led a further rebellion in 1896–1897, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> were shot during the rinderpest epidemic. He was captured and imprisoned, and the Galeshewe township in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kimberley">Kimberley</a> is named after him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/galeshewe.jpg" alt="Galeshewe" width="320" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kimberley City Info · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Greater_No_2,_Galeshewe_township.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
